app2appAsynch is the simplest case of LJMS usage for application - application
communication (asynchronous message delivery) where one Java application acts as a client and another one - as the server only
(so only one JVM here initiates message communication, although messages are
going in 2 directions while connection is established and 2 message terminals
(MessagePortal) are equal in rights: both can send messages while connection is alive
and both can close connection: assymetry here is in who can initiate and who is
waiting for a connection).
P2P node application will rather have equal rights having both client and server
parts in the same JVM, which means - it will instantiate either an instance
of org.is.net.Servlet class (server part) and LSessionImpl subclass instance
(connection initiating part of the broker).
For extending this example to such P2P node base, one should add the following
line (instantiating and starting the server part) to the client:
  (new org.is.server.MessageServlet(new MessageRouter())).startService(port);
and to write the necessary logic (what to do with Messages when they arrive).

Notice that this example uses simple LSessionRunnableImpl vs LSessionImpl which has
been used in the app2app example. 

To run example:
1.cd to the example directory;
2.start runServer.sh under Unix or runServer.bat under Win
3.start runClient.sh under Unix or runClient.bat under Win


For brevity necessary code snippets from this example are here:

SERVER:
      Servlet server=new org.is.server.MessageServlet(new TrivialChatRouter());
      server.startService(port);


CLIENT:

    LSessionRunnableImpl messageCentral;
    
    //INITIALIZE AND START THE BROKER:
    try{
      messageCentral=new LSessionRunnableImpl();
      messageCentral.setMessageTransport(new MessageSocket(host, port));
      messageCentral.setMessageListener(this);
      messageCentral.setExceptionListener(this); //if not set - printing to console
      messageCentral.connect();                  //make connection (init)
      messageCentral.start();                    //start message delivery
    }
    catch(JMSException e){
      System.err.println("Error connecting:"+e);
    }
    
    ....
    //USER CAN SEND MESSAGES FROM ANY PLACE/ANY TIME USING messageCentral (constructing any type of Message supported):

    Message ms=new MessageImpl();
    ms.setStringProperty("text",line);
    messageCentral.sendMessage(ms);

    ...

    //FREE RESOURCES (close session at the end of the work with broker):
    messageCentral.close();

